58,3,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A is too rigid and discourages newcomers, while Option C is an administrative nightmare. Option B seems the most logical compromise, provided the tiers are simple and not overly restrictive.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,I must agree with Asuka regarding Option C. It is a recipe for disaster; we would be asking our treasurer to act as a part-time debt collector for pocket change. Option A is far more elegant.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Option A is simple, but it ignores the imbalance I found in the records. Those who contribute the most effort and time shouldn't feel they are subsidizing those who never show up.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option A is the only one that removes the ""transactional"" feeling. If we go with B or C, we're just inviting people to calculate the value of their membership every time they consider leaving the house.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Takumi,,"Option A is the only way to ensure we actually cover the fixed costs. If we go with B or C, we're just guessing again and risking a deficit we can't afford.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, Option A ensures the money is there, but it doesn't solve the fact that people are refusing to join because of the upfront cost. We are trading long-term growth for short-term stability.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, while growth is a noble pursuit, it is rather difficult to grow a club that has no one willing to manage the finances. Option A protects the treasurer's sanity, which is a prerequisite for any growth.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, I understand the need for simplicity, but we must consider the human cost. If the dedicated few feel exploited by a flat fee, we risk losing the very people who keep the club running.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, the ""human cost"" of a flat fee is far lower than the psychological cost of making members feel like customers. If we start pricing our presence, we're just admitting the club is a service, not a community.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, the ""human cost"" is that the club goes bankrupt! If we don't have a guaranteed sum upfront, we can't pay the hall. I'm not risking the club's existence on a ""feeling"" of fairness.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Shunichi, if we want belonging to feel automatic, we cannot have a system where people are turned away at the door because they can't commit to a full year. Option B allows for different levels of commitment.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, while Option B sounds like a sophisticated compromise, I fear it is merely a more complex version of the same administrative trap. More tiers often mean more disputes over which ""level"" a member belongs to.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, if we keep the tiers very simple, the administrative burden should remain manageable. It allows us to acknowledge different levels of engagement without the chaos of per-event charging.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Asuka, ""different levels of commitment"" is just a polite way of saying we're categorizing our members. Once you label someone a ""low-tier"" member, you've officially turned the club into a subscription service.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Shunichi, call it whatever you want, but ""subscription services"" actually get paid! We have a third of our members struggling with renewals. Option A is the only one that gives us a clear, predictable number to work with.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, predictability is useless if the membership pool shrinks. Option B provides a way for newcomers to drift in without a daunting upfront cost, preserving the club's openness while still securing the funds we need. I vote for B.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I fear we are prioritizing the ""idea"" of accessibility over the reality of operation. Option B is a siren song of compromise that will likely end in a treasurer's resignation. I vote for A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I cannot support Option A because it ignores the resentment of those carrying the club. Option B is a gentle way to balance accessibility and fairness without the chaos of Option C. I vote for B.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"I've tried to be patient, but we're circling the drain. Option B is just a fancy way of telling people they're second-class members. If we want the energy back, we stop the accounting at the door. I vote for A.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Takumi,,"I'm not playing games with the club's survival. We can't afford ""tiers"" or ""feelings"" when the bank account is this thin and the fixed costs are that high. I vote for A.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
